# Keep the RL weight sync on the checkpoint's power-of-two FP8 scale grid.
#
# Apply with:  cd /app/verl && git apply verl-ue8m0-fp8-scale-grid.patch
# Disable at runtime with VERL_UE8M0_SCALE_FIX=0; on by default.
#
#
# The bug
# -------
# DeepSeek-V4 checkpoints declare scale_fmt: ue8m0 and store every block scale
# as a power of two. This is the format, not a coincidence: across the whole
# checkpoint, all 25 module classes -- attention (wq_a, wq_b, wkv, wo_a, wo_b,
# indexer.wq_b), the MoE experts (1792 tensors x w1/w2/w3), the shared experts
# and the MTP layer -- are 100% powers of two over 78,464 scale values, with no
# exceptions.
#
# Megatron-Bridge's FP8 export ignores that and recomputes the scale as
# amax/448, which lands back on the grid only 12.5% of the time. The fp8 codes
# were chosen for the original grid, so a shifted grid forces every weight onto
# new codes -- and e4m3 has 3 mantissa bits, so each re-rounding costs up to 6%.
#
# Measured on layer 0's wq_b:
#
#   scales still a power of two after export      12.5%
#   fp8 bytes unchanged by the round trip          6.5%
#   weight error vLLM ends up running          2.78e-02
#   weight change from one training step       ~1e-05
#
# So 99.9% of the error vLLM sees is the export, not the model changing.
#
# Nothing is lost reading the checkpoint: Megatron's bf16 weights are
# bit-identical to it (relL2 exactly 0), because fp8's 3 mantissa bits fit in
# bf16's 7 and multiplying by a power of two only shifts the exponent. The loss
# is entirely in writing back.
#
#
# The fix
# -------
# One line of arithmetic in scale_from_amax: snap the recomputed scale up to
# the nearest power of two. The scale is still an fp32 tensor, still recomputed
# from the current weights on every sync, still consumed as fp32 by the
# kernels -- only its value is constrained to the grid the checkpoint uses. The
# weights stay fp8 e4m3, untouched. The patch is gated on the source scale
# already being a power of two, so a model that does not use a ue8m0 grid is
# left alone.
#
#
# Evidence: the export error
# --------------------------
# Weight error relL2(exported, Megatron) for layer 0's wq_b, per training step,
# and the share of exported scales still on the grid:
#
#              bf16 training            fp8 training (e4m3, blockwise)
#   step    fix on    fix off         fix on    fix off
#      0    0.0       2.7846e-02      0.0       2.7846e-02
#      1    6.69e-06  2.7846e-02      6.69e-06  2.7846e-02
#      2    1.15e-05  2.7846e-02      1.13e-05  2.7846e-02
#      3    1.81e-05  --              1.81e-05  --
#   scales  100%      12.5%           100%      12.5%
#
# The two training modes agree to four digits. fp8 training leaves the export
# path alone because fp8_param is unset, so TE keeps the parameters in bf16 and
# only quantizes inside the GEMMs. With the fix the residual error is the size
# of the weight change itself, i.e. the requantization stops contributing.
#
#
# Evidence: scale coverage at runtime
# -----------------------------------
# Every block scale live in the rollout engine, fix on, fp8 training, over 3
# steps and 8 ranks -- 9,649,152 values, all powers of two:
#
#   attn.fused_wqa_wkv                        64,512   100%
#   attn.indexer.wq_b                         36,864   100%
#   attn.wo_a / wo_b / wq_b                1,032,192   100%
#   ffn.experts.routed_experts.w13         5,505,024   100%
#   ffn.experts.routed_experts.w2          2,752,512   100%
#   ffn.shared_experts.gate_up / down        258,048   100%
#
# Without the fix the same measurement gives 12.5%.
#
#
# Evidence: end to end
# --------------------
# DeepSeek-V4-Flash-Base-7L, GRPO, 8192-token responses, arms interleaved,
# n=10 per arm, exact permutation test.
#
#   bf16 training          fix on      fix off     delta        p
#     rollout_corr/kl      0.004587    0.008297    -44.7%   <0.0001
#     probs_diff_mean      0.009935    0.012822    -22.5%   <0.0001
#     probs_diff_max       0.315580    0.402501    -21.6%   <0.0001
#     time_per_step         146.3 s     145.8 s     +0.4%     0.25
#
#   fp8 training           fix on      fix off     delta        p
#     rollout_corr/kl      0.008696    0.012331    -29.5%   <0.0001
#     probs_diff_mean      0.013586    0.015895    -14.5%   <0.0001
#     probs_diff_max       0.406810    0.487854    -16.6%   <0.0001
#     time_per_step         147.9 s     147.2 s     +0.5%    0.025
#
#   kl per run, bf16, fix on:  0.004086 0.004253 0.004489 0.004578 0.004643
#                              0.004683 0.004684 0.004686 0.004840 0.004932
#   kl per run, bf16, fix off: 0.007758 0.007773 0.007992 0.008103 0.008275
#                              0.008382 0.008522 0.008599 0.008686 0.008880
#
# Neither comparison overlaps: the worst run with the fix beats the best run
# without it. The absolute gain is the same in both training modes (0.0037 vs
# 0.0036); the ratio is smaller under fp8 only because fp8 training adds ~0.004
# of its own gap, which this fix does not address.
#
#
# Limits worth knowing
# --------------------
# Snapping *up* means the largest weight in a block maps to somewhere in
# [224, 448] instead of exactly 448, giving up one bit of fp8 range. On a
# tensor that is not already on the grid that is a small loss, so this is not a
# general "powers of two are better" claim -- it wins here only because the
# weights are still sitting on the checkpoint's grid.
#
# How long that holds is not measured. Sweeping simulated drift, the fix's
# error tracks the drift itself (1e-04 drift -> 1e-04 error) while amax/448
# stays flat at ~2.9e-02, and the two only meet at ~5e-02 relative drift. At
# the observed ~1e-05 per step that is thousands of steps away, but the real
# evidence covers 5 steps at lr 1e-6. For a long post-training run, log
# relL2(exported weight, Megatron weight) and watch it.
#
# All of the above is on the 7L debug model. The production 43L model has more
# layers for the error to accumulate through and sat at a higher KL to begin
# with, so the effect there is unmeasured and could differ in either direction.
